---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = 'About Apostille Atlas and how we research';
const description = 'What Apostille Atlas covers, how each fee and filing rule is sourced, and how the site makes money.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-26">
  <h1>About Apostille Atlas</h1>
  <p class="lede">A reference for people who need a U.S. apostille or authentication certificate: which office issues it, what it costs, and whether you can mail the request or walk in, state by state.</p>
  <p>Apostille Atlas is an independent comparison guide published by Atlas Guides. For questions, see <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>.</p>
  <h2>How we source things</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fees, office addresses, mail versus in-person rules, and processing statements come only from the official secretary of state, department of state, or other competent-authority page, with a link next to every figure.</li>
    <li>The difference between an apostille and an authentication certificate is described from those official pages and from the Hague Conference on Private International Law (HCCH) Apostille Section.</li>
    <li>If an official page does not publish a figure, such as a mail turnaround time, we show a dash or say the office does not publish one, rather than guessing.</li>
    <li>Every page shows the date we last checked it. Fees and counter rules change; follow the linked official page before you file or pay.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What this site is not</h2>
  <p>It isn't legal advice, and it isn't a government office. We don't issue apostilles, notarize documents, or file requests ourselves. If you'd like someone to handle it, the <a href="/get-it-done/">request form</a> passes your details to an independent apostille service, which quotes and does the work. Destination countries can add their own rules, and some offices will not apostille a document that is missing a required earlier certification. Check the linked official page or a local attorney before you act.</p>
  <h2>How the site makes money</h2>
  <p>We have affiliate relationships with Notarize, BlueNotary, and NotaryLive; other online-notarization providers are not active affiliates today. See the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>. Affiliate status will never change a fee, a fact, or the order of a table. An online notary platform does not issue the apostille itself.</p>
  <p>When you use the <a href="/get-it-done/">request form</a>, we pass your request to an independent apostille service, and that service may pay us a referral fee. That never changes what you pay the service.</p>
</Base>
